      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Showing on the nameplate if the player has instance moderation power isn't the worst idea. But it could also be a good idea to not show it maybe? I am on the fence about this.\n\nCurrently anyone that actually has moderation powers in an instance can prove it by using the \"Warn\" moderation action on someone.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-12-01T03:58:47.787Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "There should be some sort of indication of how much \"power\" a user has within a group, maybe shown by an icon on the group section of the nameplate. \n\nRoles that allow a user to kick/ban players from the group, invite players, or other big ticket permissions should be easily viewable to others. \n\nThis helps with group dynamic as it establishes some leadership and authority roles, as well as lets outside viewers decide on if they want to join a group or not based on the actions and power of someone in said group.\n\nThese icons could even only show up in group instances or for other members of the group if generally broadcasting these power roles is an issue. But similar to something like discord, its important to know when someone can actually follow through on some action they are threatening. It adds a layer of seriousness when a troublemaker knows the person talking to them can and will do something about their actions if they continue. While also protecting group members from people pretending they have the power to do something they don't.\n\nin addition If someone is being annoying, breaking group rules or otherwise being problematic in a group, being able to immediately pick out a member with the right permissions to deal with the issue seems like a good idea.",
